From eed2eb44ed20f19e3312d864fdebb6cdefd609ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Murka-Moncz Zsigmond <sanyikapenz@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 00:28:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Hash strings with hassh64 instead of FNV-1a

hashString() walked the string a byte at a time with 32-bit FNV-1a.
Replace it with hassh64 (vendored as src/vm/hassh.h, MIT; the notice is
in the header so it also lands in the amalgamation), folded to the
32-bit hash ObjString stores. Numbers and other values hash as before.

Hash function alone, gcc -O2, ns per key:

                                          FNV-1a  hassh64
  identifiers in wren_core.wren (6.7 B)    2.08     1.72
  identifiers in test/ (10.6 B)            3.23     1.89
  map_string keys (22.8 B)                 8.24     2.65
  44 B strings                            19.44     3.04
  132 B strings                           76.84     7.80
  1-4 B identifiers                        1.31     1.60

test/benchmark, release_64bit, best of 15 interleaved runs, ms:

                    before after
  map_string         71.7  69.4  (-3.2%)
  binary_trees_gc   374.1 354.3  (-5.3%)
  string_equals      83.5  83.5
  method_call        49.8  49.2
  others within +-2%

All 866 tests pass in the four CI configurations and release_32bit, and
the raw output of every test script is unchanged.
